{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T17:57:03Z","timestamp":1790791023457,"version":"4.1.0"},"reference-count":66,"publisher":"Noyam Publishers","issue":"8","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,28]],"date-time":"2026-09-28T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1790553600000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["ERATS"],"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper examines syncretism in African Pentecostalism using a decolonial pneumatological lens. African Pentecostalism, Charismatics, and neo-Pentecostal movements pursue a distinct pneumatological spirituality, rooted in both indigenous cosmologies and global Pentecostal theology. This article employed qualitative desktop research and used syncretism as a conceptual framework. The findings reveal a departure from Western critiques which frame syncretic practices as theological compromise.  Thus, African Pentecostal syncretism uses a Spirit-led negotiation between indigenous cosmology and global Pentecostal theology. The study concludes that syncretism in African Pentecostalism is a decolonial negotiation, not theological compromise. A pneumatological hermeneutic validates hybrid practices, providing a platform for prayers and deliverance against ancestral influences. The article recommends a pneumatological hermeneutic that validates hybrid spiritual practices as legitimate expressions of faith that are tailored to reclaim African agency and militate against Eurocentric ideas of pure Christianity. This study contributes to reconceptualises syncretism as a decolonial practice in African Pentecostalism.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.38159\/erats.20261281","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]],"date-time":"2026-09-30T17:16:03Z","timestamp":1790788563000},"page":"332","up